{
  "case_id": "2003-03-107",
  "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0000-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: We'll hear argument now in No. 03-107, the United States v. Billy Jo Lara. Mr. Kneedler."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Why didn't -- why didn't they extend it to non-Indians? I mean, if it's a problem when a -- a non-member Indian commits an offense on an Indian reservation, why isn't it an equivalent problem when a -- a white man commits the same crime on an Indian reservation?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It -- generally, it has been understood to require a tribal affiliation. First of all, the definition under -- under the Indian Civil Rights Act for tribal power, Congress adopted the same meaning of Indian that is applied under the Federal criminal statutes for the purpose of having the two mesh completely."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Mr. Kneedler, is it the case that the Bill of Rights is -- has not been thought applicable to criminal defendants who are members of the tribe in a tribal court or to non-tribal Indian -- non-tribal member Indians?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, if it's authorized -- exercising its own power, how then can Congress make the Bill of Rights applicable. As -- as a condition to the exercise of sovereignty? Is that the way it works?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0000-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, let's -- let's apply that across the board then. I guess Congress can lift the Fifth Amendment?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0000-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Authorized States."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0000-0010",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: -- because Congress -- Congress can redefine the term dependent?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0000-0011",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: And -- and since this has nothing to do with prior Indian tribal power but simply with arrangements that Congress chooses to adopt, it necessarily follows that Congress could provide that anyone in this room, whether an Indian -- whether an enrolled Indian or not, could be subjected to trial by a -- a tribal court and then subjected to a second trial, despite the Double Jeopardy Clause, by a court of the United States. Right? Congress could provide that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0000-0012",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: That provides a limitation. That provides a limitation to -- you know, to jurisdiction over the -- over the tribal members, and that was a very important limitation. Now you're saying that limitation doesn't exist."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: This is maybe -- it's very interesting. Suppose -- I would think on your approach then that the rights that the individual tribe gets is a function of the Due Process Clause."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Kneedler",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, with -- first of all, with respect to members of tribes other than the prosecuting tribe, Congress in the exercise of its plenary authority over tribes we think certainly should have the power to regulate the relationship among tribes, tribes with each other, and relationships of tribes and their respective members with each other."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Kneedler",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "provide a vehicle for that argument."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Reporter",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ALEXANDER F. REICHERT ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Reichert",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When Congress amended the Indian Civil rights Act in response to this Court's invitation in Duro, Congress exceeded its power by overruling this -- this Court's determination on the limits of tribal sovereignty."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: What transformed the tribes from independent sovereigns to dependent sovereigns was not a decision of this Court and the act of Congress. It was the acts of the legislature and they took over a lot of things that were previously independent sovereignty of the tribes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court transcript",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What transformed the tribes from independent sovereigns to dependent sovereigns was not a decision of this Court and the act of Congress. It was the acts of the legislature and they took over a lot of things that were previously independent sovereignty of the tribes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Reichert (Government)",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. REICHERT: Congress has specific enumerated powers in the Constitution. Nowhere in those specific enumerated powers -- and the Government has pointed to no specific enumerated powers that allows Congress to take the action they did."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Because my next question would be if they did, could there then be double jeopardy if there was a second trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Kneeldler",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KNEEDLER: That -- that would -- that -- that might present a -- a separate problem, but if the -- because there -- it might be the same offense."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: Thank you, Mr. Kneedler. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2003-03-107",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99e44637c71572b4855477d7109db8a5a9d308f062b1f0fda2eb8f826f514fa9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99e44637c715-0003-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "(Whereupon, at 11:09 a.m., the case in the above-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
